the target language is: prolog.

% Demonstrates the ';synonym' template addition. A template may declare one (or
% more) equivalent surface forms after '; synonym'; every form maps to the SAME
% Prolog predicate, so facts, rule heads, rule bodies and queries may be written
% with the main form or any synonym interchangeably. Explanations render the main
% (first) form by default.
%
% To simplify things, a template with a synonym cannot carry any other addition.

the templates are:
    *a person* is happy; synonym *a person* is content.
    *a person* deserves a reward; synonym *a person* earns a prize.
    *a person* is rich; undefined.
    *a person* is loved; undefined.

the knowledge base synonyms includes:
    A person is happy
        if the person is rich.

    % head written with the 'is content' synonym — same predicate as 'is happy'
    A person is content
        if the person is loved.

    % body written with the 'is content' synonym
    A person deserves a reward
        if the person is content.

scenario friends is:
    alice is rich.
    bob is loved.
    dave is content.                       % scenario fact written with a synonym
    % Each query renders its answers with the surface form used in the query itself.
    happy expects answers ["alice is happy", "bob is happy", "dave is happy"].
    content expects answers ["alice is content", "bob is content", "dave is content"].
    reward expects answers ["alice deserves a reward", "bob deserves a reward", "dave deserves a reward"].
    prize expects answers ["alice earns a prize", "bob earns a prize", "dave earns a prize"].

query happy is:
    which person is happy.

query content is:
    which person is content.

query reward is:
    which person deserves a reward.

query prize is:
    which person earns a prize.
